body: "Found by running the demos against a real 664-item workspace.\n\n## The inconsistency\n\nThree demos require an argument. Two validate it; the third does not:\n\n```console\n$ pm ts-starter search-demo\n  pm-ts-starter: search-demo requires a --query (or positional query) argument.     # clear\n\n$ pm ts-starter history-compact-demo\n  pm-ts-starter: history-compact-demo requires an --id (or positional id) argument.  # clear\n\n$ pm ts-starter plan-demo\n  pm-ts-starter: `plan` demo failed (pm exited 2): {\n    \"type\": \"urn:pm-cli:error:invalid_argument_value\",\n    \"detail\": \"pm plan show requires a plan id\",\n    ...20 more lines of nested JSON...\n  }\n```\n\n`plan-demo` shells out as `pm --pm-path <path> plan show --depth standard --json` with no plan id, lets pm reject it, and forwards pm's entire error payload wrapped in its own.\n\n## Why it matters here specifically\n\nThis package exists to **demonstrate correct SDK usage to extension authors**. Its own demos are the worked examples, so an inconsistency between them is a teaching defect rather than a cosmetic one: a reader comparing the three learns that argument validation is optional and that forwarding a nested error payload is an acceptable house style.\n\nIt is also the worst of the three to read. The useful sentence — `pm plan show requires a plan id` — is on line 4 of an embedded JSON blob, under a message that says the *demo* failed rather than that an argument is missing.\n\n## Suggested fix\n\nValidate up front, in the same voice as the siblings:\n\n```\npm-ts-starter: plan-demo requires a --plan (or positional plan id) argument.\n```\n\nOr, if the demo is meant to be runnable with no arguments, have it resolve a plan itself (list plans, take the first, and say which it chose) so the zero-argument path demonstrates something rather than failing.\n\n## Side effect worth knowing about\n\nBecause `plan-demo` places `--pm-path` **before** the subcommand, the forwarded payload also carries a malformed suggestion — `pm <path> --help`, the flag's value used as the command. That is an upstream defect in how pm derives the example, filed as [unbraind/pm-cli#984](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-cli/issues/984); it surfaces here because extensions build argv with global flags first. Validating the argument locally avoids forwarding it at all.\n\n## What worked\n\nThe rest of the package behaved correctly against a real workspace: `info` reports name, version and the SDK target it was built against; `context-demo` produced a real context snapshot over 664 items; and the two sibling demos give exemplary usage errors — which is precisely why the third stands out.\n\n---\n\n### GitHub comments (1)\n\n> **@coderabbitai[bot]** (2026-08-12T01:36:12Z)\n>\n> <!-- This is an auto-generated issue plan by CodeRabbit -->\n> <details>\n> <summary>🔗 Related PRs</summary>\n> \n> unbraind/pm-ts-starter#39 - Adopt the full SDK authoring builder surface and fix a fabricated vector-store adapter contract [merged]\n> unbraind/pm-ts-starter#43 - fix(output-format): decline the demo override so it stops reprinting the host envelope [merged]\n> </details>\n> \n> ---\n> <details>\n> <summary>📝 Issue Planner</summary>\n> \n> <sub>Check the box below or use the `@coderabbitai plan` command to generate an implementation plan and prompts that you can use with your favorite coding assistant.</sub>\n> \n> - [ ] <!-- {\"checkboxId\": \"8d4f2b9c-3e1a-4f7c-a9b2-d5e8f1c4a7b9\"} --> Create Plan\n> </details>\n> \n> \n> ---\n> <details>\n> <summary> 🧪 Issue enrichment is currently in open beta.</summary>\n> \n> \n> You can configure auto-planning by selecting labels in the issue_enrichment configuration.\n> \n> To disable automatic issue enrichment, add the following to your `.coderabbit.yaml`:\n> ```yaml\n> issue_enrichment:\n>   auto_enrich:\n>     enabled: false\n> ```\n> </details>\n> \n> 💬 Have feedback or questions? Drop into our [discord](https://discord.gg/coderabbit)!"
